{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791563040,"nonce":"hTfggpA/9DkHtOo44at2mBR1OXY=","message":"Root cause across the largest gaps (local, arm64 M-series, shipped llvm,aot build, LuaJIT 2.1 JIT on). Each kernel was run 5 times in one process: pass 1 is what the bench measures, passes 2-5 are steady state.\n\n| kernel | pass 1 vs luajit | steady vs luajit | warm-up cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| spectral_norm | 2.90x | 1.23x | ~19 ms |\n| nbody | 1.75x | 0.56x | ~73 ms |\n| fasta | 1.67x | 0.81x | ~13 ms |\n| k_nucleotide | 2.03x | 1.59x | ~22 ms |\n| reverse_complement | 2.12x | 1.54x | ~6 ms |\n| pidigits | 2.04x | 1.82x | ~53 ms |\n| regex_redux | 4.31x | 3.35x | ~41 ms |\n| fannkuch_redux | 2.64x | 2.39x | ~75 ms |\n\n1. Warm-up is most of the gap: the first pass runs on the Cranelift optimized tier, because the LLVM tier arrives late or never (WLIFT_TIER_TRACE). nbody's advance gets LLVM at 98 ms of a 102 ms run (opt 15-18 ms + codegen 32-62 ms). spectral_norm, fasta and reverse_complement queue LLVM and never install it. k_nucleotide's single LLVM thread compiles the untimed setup function `sequence` first. pidigits queues at 169 ms.\n   - LLVM's latency is real, not a WrenLift setup cost: llc -O2 on the same module (sequence, 388 lines) spends ~10 ms in codegen passes alone (ISel 2.6 ms, machine scheduler 2 ms, greedy RA 1.1 ms). CodeGen O0 is 4-6 ms but its code is ~3x slower.\n   - The LLVM thread runs at QOS_CLASS_UTILITY on macOS (nice 10 on Linux), but normal priority only moved codegen 48 -\u003e 36 ms.\n   - So the tier that must be good early is Cranelift. Cranelift vs LLVM steady state: nbody 1.96x, fasta 1.73x, spectral 1.60x, fannkuch 1.53x, regex 1.50x, k_nucleotide 1.38x, pidigits 1.15x, reverse_complement 1.01x.\n2. Steady state, both tiers, against LuaJIT (micro: 1000-element List, ns/element): read LLVM 1.05 / Cranelift 1.8 / LuaJIT 0.98; write 1.8-2.0 / 1.7 / 0.61; swap 3.4 / 3.4 / 1.42. List writes are ~3x LuaJIT in both tiers, and every gap kernel writes lists in its hot loop.\n\nWhat the Cranelift loops spend it on (machine code via WLIFT_JIT_CODE_DIR):\n- A loop-carried 16-byte spill: `ldr q16,[sp,#off]` at the top and `str q16,[sp,#off]` at the bottom of every inner iteration, for the outer counter. It is an f64 block parameter the inner loop only passes through, kept for the deopt exits. The reload depends on the previous store, so the iteration is bound by store-to-load forwarding. The same pattern is in the read loop.\n- The spill offsets were 8 mod 16 (Cranelift's spill area follows the word-rounded explicit slots; see Zyntax 9bc3fcd0, same Cranelift rev bc2f967).\n- Per write: elem_class store; NaN-box check of the stored f64; count reload for the bounds check.\n- Before the change below, every write also had a `wren_subscript_set` call on its slow path, which forced a spill and a post-merge error poll onto the hot path.\n- Two safepoint/error polls per iteration, each rebuilding a 64-bit address with mov/movk.\n\nUncommitted, pending x86-64 measurement on the NUC:\n- Cranelift SubscriptSet into a guarded List takes every value inline (objects fold into elem_class as ObjList::note_element does) and leaves by deopt (the SlowPathExit speculate_call_results already places) on a bad index. The call and the error poll leave the loop; arm64 micro unchanged.\n- align_spill_area: a zero-sized 16-aligned explicit slot before define_function (JIT and AOT). Spills move to 16-aligned offsets with immediate addressing; arm64 unchanged; Zyntax measured FFT -19% on x86-64.\n\nTried and reverted: leaving pass-through loop parameters boxed in UnboxParams (all params with one source: reads/writes 1.7 -\u003e 4.6 ns; loop-carried only: no Cranelift change, LLVM writes looked worse).","files":null}]}